+# Retrieval options
+
+Revision 1 — 2026-10-02.
+
+This comparison supports a future experiment with read-only retrieval of synthetic
+local data, with no UI. PostgreSQL is selected; application language and interface
+remain deferred. The entries below are planning assumptions, not measured results.
+Production suitability and performance remain unverified.
+
+## Compare the options
+
+Each row describes one way to exercise retrieval after the
+[open model decisions](model.md#decisions-blocking-implementation) are settled.
+
+| Option | What it exposes | What it requires | What needs maintenance |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Saved SQL queries | Queries and joins directly | A SQL-capable caller | Query definitions as the schema changes |
+| Command-line application | Repeatable, named retrieval operations | Application language and packaging choices | Query code as the schema changes |
+| HTTP endpoint | Retrieval through requests from separate callers | Transport, access and hosting decisions, plus operational work | Request contracts and query code |
+
+There is no present requirement for separate callers. The HTTP option therefore
+adds decisions and operational work without a current need for that capability.
+A command-line application would make named operations repeatable, but its
+language and packaging choices remain open.
+
+## Recommendation for the next experiment
+
+First resolve the domain decisions owned by the
+[provisional database model](model.md#decisions-blocking-implementation). Once
+those decisions are settled, use saved SQL queries for a small, proposed retrieval
+experiment: their direct view of joins suits checking how the model answers the
+retrieval questions, and they require no application language choice.
+
+Limit that experiment to the model's two questions on a small synthetic local
+dataset: which agreements involve an organisation, and which services each
+agreement covers. Compare the returned identities with expected results and
+record whether the query definitions are understandable and maintainable. Those
+observations would inform whether named command-line operations are useful later;
+separate callers would need a concrete requirement before reconsidering HTTP.
+
+This recommendation selects no application stack and creates no implementation.
+Provisioning, migrations, seed loading, runtime checks and container packaging
+remain outside this documentation task. A successful small retrieval experiment
+would establish neither production suitability nor performance at other scales.
